A `Roster` object stores `Student` objects in an `ArrayList<Student>` instance variable named `students`. The `Student` class has the methods `String getName()` and `int getGrade()`.

```java
public class Roster {
    private ArrayList<Student> students;

    /** Returns a new ArrayList containing the names of the students whose grade is at least
     *  threshold, in the order the students appear in students. Does not change students.
     *  Returns an empty list if no student qualifies. */
    public ArrayList<String> namesAtOrAbove(int threshold)
    { /* to be implemented in part (a) */ }

    /** Removes from students every student whose grade is less than cutoff, keeping the
     *  remaining students in their original order. Returns the number of students removed. */
    public int removeBelow(int cutoff)
    { /* to be implemented in part (b) */ }
}
```

**(a)** Write the method `namesAtOrAbove`.

**(b)** Write the method `removeBelow`. Be careful that every qualifying student is removed, including two such students in a row.

sampleResponse:
```java
public ArrayList<String> namesAtOrAbove(int threshold) {
    ArrayList<String> result = new ArrayList<String>();
    for (Student s : students) {
        if (s.getGrade() >= threshold) {
            result.add(s.getName());
        }
    }
    return result;
}

public int removeBelow(int cutoff) {
    int removed = 0;
    for (int i = students.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        if (students.get(i).getGrade() < cutoff) {
            students.remove(i);
            removed++;
        }
    }
    return removed;
}
```
In `removeBelow`, traversing from the end means a removal shifts only elements that have already been examined. (Traversing forward also works if the index is decremented after each removal.)

