An `Inventory` object stores `Item` objects in an `ArrayList<Item>` instance variable named `items`. The `Item` class has the following methods.

```java
public class Item {
    public Item(String name, int qty)   // creates an item with the given name and quantity
    public String getName()
    public int getQty()
    public void setQty(int q)
}
```

The `Inventory` class contains the two methods described below.

```java
public class Inventory {
    private ArrayList<Item> items;

    /** If an item named name is in items, adds amount to its quantity. Otherwise adds a new
     *  Item with that name and the quantity amount to the end of items. At most one item
     *  in items has any given name. */
    public void restock(String name, int amount)
    { /* to be implemented in part (a) */ }

    /** Removes every item whose quantity is 0, keeping the other items in their original
     *  order, and returns the number of items removed. */
    public int removeEmpty()
    { /* to be implemented in part (b) */ }
}
```

**(a)** Write the method `restock`.

**(b)** Write the method `removeEmpty`. Be careful that no item with quantity 0 is skipped.

sampleResponse:
```java
public void restock(String name, int amount) {
    for (Item it : items) {
        if (it.getName().equals(name)) {
            it.setQty(it.getQty() + amount);
            return;
        }
    }
    items.add(new Item(name, amount));
}

public int removeEmpty() {
    int count = 0;
    int i = 0;
    while (i < items.size()) {
        if (items.get(i).getQty() == 0) {
            items.remove(i);
            count++;
        } else {
            i++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}
```
In `restock`, the item is changed through its `setQty` method while the enhanced `for` loop is running; the loop only reads the list, so this is safe, and `return` ends the method before the new item is added. In `removeEmpty`, the index advances only when nothing was removed, so the element that shifts into position `i` is examined.
